/**
@page cpp_api Using the C++ Library

@tableofcontents

Most Mercury games are projects: their maps, assets and UI are data, and their
gameplay is AngelScript, C++, or both. AngelScript projects run on the shared
player; C++ projects build their own executable with `mercury_add_game`. This page
explains the C++ interface for writing C++ scripts, extending the engine,
writing tools and tests, or embedding the player in another executable.

@section cpp_api_entry The player entry point

`mercury_player` is a thin wrapper around @ref Mercury::RunPlayer.

@code{.cpp}
// mercury/player/main.cc
#include "player/player.h"

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  return Mercury::RunPlayer(argc, argv, nullptr);
}
@endcode

@ref Mercury::RunPlayer loads a `.mproj`, compiles and runs its AngelScript
scripts, and creates a @ref Mercury::MapScene. C++ scripts are already
registered by the time `main()` runs; the player leaves those types in place.
Passing a default project makes a dedicated executable that runs your game
when it is started without arguments:

@code{.cpp}
#include "player/player.h"

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  return Mercury::RunPlayer(argc, argv, "my_game/my_game.mproj");
}
@endcode

Add it with `mercury_add_game` in the game's `CMakeLists.txt`:

@code{.cmake}
mercury_add_game(my_game PROJECT "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}")
@endcode

@section cpp_api_layers The layers

| Type | Role |
|---|---|
| @ref Mercury::Application | The platform layer: GLFW window, input, frame loop, fixed ticks. |
| @ref Mercury::VulkanApplication | The Vulkan renderer implementation of the application hooks. `Mercury::DefaultApplication` is an alias for it. |
| @ref Mercury::GameScene | The contract between game state and the renderer. |
| @ref Mercury::MapScene | A `GameScene` built from a `.mmap`; runs the map's @ref Mercury::Behaviour objects. |
| @ref Mercury::RenderHost | What a scene may ask the application to render. Used by the editor for viewports. |
| @ref Mercury::Project | A `.mproj` file and its `res://` path resolution. |
| @ref Mercury::AssetDatabase | Loads and caches `.masset` assets. |
| @ref Mercury::Behaviour | The base class the engine runs; scripts are driven by `ScriptBehaviour`. |
| @ref Mercury::EditorUI::MercuryEditorApp | The wxWidgets editor application. |

The application owns the loop and the scene owns the game state. The scene
never touches Vulkan or GLFW: it exposes lights, cameras and draw data that
the application turns into GPU work. This keeps the renderer replaceable and
lets the editor render the same scene into an offscreen viewport.

@section cpp_api_scene Hosting a custom scene

A custom scene derives from @ref Mercury::GameScene and implements its four
pure virtual callbacks. @ref Mercury::VulkanApplication (aliased as
`Mercury::DefaultApplication`) supplies the window and renderer:

@code{.cpp}
#include <memory>

#include "application/default_application.h"
#include "scene/scene.h"

class MyScene : public Mercury::GameScene {
 public:
  void OnStart() override {
    // Create entities, camera, lights and assets here.
  }
  void OnTick(double dt) override {
    // Fixed-step simulation. dt is in seconds.
  }
  void OnFrame(double dt) override {
    // Per-frame work that is not simulation.
  }
  void OnDestroy() override {}
};

int main() {
  Mercury::DefaultApplication app("My Mercury Game", 1280, 720);
  app.SetGameScene(std::make_unique<MyScene>());
  return app.Run();
}
@endcode

@ref Mercury::Application::Run calls `StartHosted()` (initialise the device,
call `OnLoad()` and `GameScene::OnStart()`), then steps frames until the
scene quits or the window closes. Use @ref Mercury::Application::StepFrame
when another framework owns the event loop, and
@ref Mercury::Application::EnableHeadlessMode when there is no window at all
(the editor uses this).

For a map-driven game, prefer @ref Mercury::MapScene. It loads a
@ref Mercury::Map, creates the physics world and render resources, and runs
the `[script]` behaviours:

@code{.cpp}
#include "project/map.h"
#include "project/project.h"
#include "scene/map_scene.h"

std::string error;
auto project = Mercury::Project::Load("my_game/my_game.mproj", &error);
auto scene = std::make_unique<Mercury::MapScene>(
    project, Mercury::MapScene::Mode::kPlay);

Mercury::Map map;
if (map.Load(project->Resolve(project->GetMainMap()), &error)) {
  scene->Instantiate(map);
}
@endcode

@section cpp_api_scripts C++ scripts

The same @ref Mercury::Behaviour a custom scene runs can be used as game
scripting: register a subclass with `MERCURY_SCRIPT` and put it on a map node
by name. This is the C++ counterpart of an AngelScript `[script]` class:

@code{.cpp}
class Spinner : public Mercury::Behaviour {
 public:
  void Reflect(Mercury::Reflector& r) override {
    r.Field("speed", speed).Tooltip("Degrees per second");
  }
  void OnFrame(double dt) override {
    GetTransform().ChangeRotate(
        {0.0f, speed * static_cast<float>(dt), 0.0f});
  }
  float speed = 45.0f;
};
MERCURY_SCRIPT(Spinner);
@endcode

`Reflect` and `ReflectData` feed the Inspector, map files, UI bindings and
Events; `ReflectMethods` makes methods callable from Events. `MERCURY_DATA_ASSET`
registers a reflected struct as a `.masset` asset type.

A game executable needs the script sources linked in. The `mercury_add_game`
CMake function does that and generates the `main()`; `[build] target` in the
project tells the editor which target to build:

@code{.cmake}
mercury_add_game(my_game PROJECT "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}")
@endcode

@code{.ini}
[build]
target = "my_game"
@endcode

Projects with only `.as` scripts keep using `mercury_player`; both languages
can coexist in one project. `examples/cpp_hello` is a complete native example.


@section cpp_api_depths Two depths of C++

C++ gameplay has two scopes, and they use different extension points:

| Depth | Use it for | Base class / registration |
|---|---|---|
| A faster AngelScript | A component with fields and callbacks, ported line by line from an `.as` script | @ref Mercury::Behaviour + `MERCURY_SCRIPT` |
| Whole features | Networking, replication, streaming, custom rendering, process setup | @ref Mercury::System, @ref Mercury::AppHook, @ref Mercury::EventBus, @ref Mercury::RenderHooks |

Both write the same `.mmap` components and reflect the same fields. They can
coexist in one project and on one node.

@section cpp_api_behaviour C++ as a faster AngelScript

This is the smallest script. The AngelScript version:

@code{.cpp}
// scripts/player_mover.as
[script]
class PlayerMover : Behaviour
{
    [field tooltip="Units per second"]
    float speed = 8.0f;

    void onStart() { Debug::Log("PlayerMover ready"); }

    void onTick(double dt)
    {
        GameObject self = GetGameObject();
        self.position = self.position + Vector3(speed * dt, 0.0f, 0.0f);
    }
}
@endcode

The C++ version writes the same class name in `src/`:

@code{.cpp}
// src/player_mover.h
#pragma once

#include <scripting/behaviour.h>

class PlayerMover : public Mercury::Behaviour {
 public:
  // The counterpart of [field tooltip="..."].
  void Reflect(Mercury::Reflector& r) override {
    r.Field("speed", speed_).Tooltip("Units per second");
  }

  void OnStart() override { PDebug::info("PlayerMover ready"); }

  void OnTick(double dt) override {
    const glm::vec3 position = GetTransform().GetPosition();
    GetTransform().SetPosition(
        position + glm::vec3(speed_ * static_cast<float>(dt), 0.0f, 0.0f));
  }

 private:
  float speed_ = 8.0f;
};
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
// src/player_mover.cc
#include "player_mover.h"

// Registers the class as a component named "PlayerMover".
MERCURY_SCRIPT(PlayerMover);
@endcode

The callback names are the C++ spellings of the AngelScript ones:
`Awake`, `OnEnable`, `OnStart`, `OnFrame`, `OnLateFrame`, `OnTick`,
`OnCollisionEnter`, `OnTriggerEnter`, `OnDestroy`. `Reflect` supports the same
field types as `[field]` (`bool`, `int`, `float`, `std::string`, `glm::vec2`,
`glm::vec3`, a `glm::vec4` Color, `NodeRef`, `AssetRef`, their arrays, and
`Enum`), plus `ReflectData` for computed UI bindings and `ReflectMethods` for
Event targets. `MERCURY_DATA_ASSET(Type)` registers a reflected struct as a
`.masset` type.

The collision/trigger callbacks take a `GameObject`; `GetGameObject()`,
`GetTransform()`, `GetComponent<T>()`, `Find`, `Instantiate`, `Physics()` and
the input helpers work the same way as in AngelScript. Events saved in a map
can call a method registered with `ReflectMethods`.

@section cpp_api_layout Project layout

@code{.text}
my_game/
  my_game.mproj        project settings; [build] target names the executable
  maps/*.mmap          maps and their components
  assets/*.masset      materials, physics materials, data assets
  scripts/*.as         AngelScript gameplay (optional)
  src/*.cc, *.h        C++ gameplay and runtime systems (optional, static)
  system/*.cc, *.h     editor extension modules (optional, dynamic)
  CMakeLists.txt       generated once, then owned by the project
  CMakePresets.json    generated once, then owned by the project
  .clangd              generated on every project open
  .mercury/            generated: schema, stamp, build trees (never committed)
  .gitignore           ignores the generated files
@endcode

`src/` is linked into the game executable; `system/` is built as a dynamic
module the editor loads. Use the editor's **New Script** dialog (**Create >
Script...**, **Add Component > New Script...**, or **Create > C++ System...**
/ **Editor System...**) to create the right pair of files in the right folder.
The dialog previews the paths, refuses to overwrite, and "Open after creating"
opens the `.cc`/`.as`.

@section cpp_api_systems Whole features: systems, app hooks and the event bus

A @ref Mercury::Behaviour is one instance per component. When a feature is
one instance per *scene* — networking, replication, replay, streaming — derive
from @ref Mercury::System and register it with `MERCURY_SYSTEM`:

@code{.cpp}
#include <scripting/system.h>
#include <scene/map_scene.h>

struct PeerConnected { int peer = 0; };

class NetworkSystem : public Mercury::System {
 public:
  // kPreTick receives before gameplay; kPostPhysics sends after physics.
  Phase GetPhase() const override { return Phase::kPostPhysics; }

  void OnRegister(Mercury::MapScene& scene) override {
    events_ = scene.GetEventBus().Subscribe<PeerConnected>(
        [this](const PeerConnected& event) { peers_.push_back(event.peer); });
  }
  void OnMapLoaded(Mercury::MapScene& scene) override {
    // The scene has entities; initialise replication state here.
  }
  void OnTick(Mercury::MapScene& scene, double dt) override { Send(dt); }

 private:
  void Send(double) {}
  Mercury::EventBus::Subscription events_;
  std::vector<int> peers_;
};

MERCURY_SYSTEM(NetworkSystem);
@endcode

Phases run in this order inside a fixed tick: `kPreTick` (before collision
dispatch and behaviours, e.g. network receive), `kTick` (after behaviours),
`kPostTick` (after script timers, before physics), `kPostPhysics` (after the
physics step, e.g. network send). `GetOrder()` breaks ties inside a phase.
Lifecycle: `OnRegister` once, `OnMapLoaded`/`OnMapUnloading` per map, the
frame callbacks every frame, `OnTick` every fixed tick, `OnShutdown` once.

**EventBus** is the scene-wide typed publish/subscribe bus used above:
`scene.GetEventBus().Publish(event)` and
`Subscribe<E>(callback)`. A `Subscription` is a move-only RAII handle; destroying
it unsubscribes, and a handle that outlives the bus is harmless. The bus is
not thread-safe; publish from the scene thread.

**AppHook** is process-wide setup: parse game arguments, open sockets, start
services. It runs only for a real game run, never for the editor's script
build or `--dump-schema`:

@code{.cpp}
#include <scripting/app_hook.h>

class ServerHook : public Mercury::AppHook {
 public:
  bool OnPreInit(std::span<const std::string_view> args) override {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < args.size(); i++) {
      if (args[i] == "--server") server_ = true;
      if (args[i] == "--port" && i + 1 < args.size()) port_ = args[i + 1];
    }
    return true;
  }
  bool OnInit() override { return !server_ || listener_.Listen(port_); }
  void OnShutdown() override { listener_.Close(); }

 private:
  bool server_ = false;
  std::string port_ = "7777";
  Listener listener_;
};

MERCURY_APP_HOOK(ServerHook);
@endcode

Unknown `--options` are accepted by the player only when at least one
`AppHook` is registered; the hook sees the raw arguments. Put the project
path before game-specific options:
`./my_game my_game.mproj --server --port 9000`.

**Asset importers.** @ref Mercury::AssetImporterRegistry maps file extensions
to asset types. Register an importer owned by an editor module with
`RegisterOwned(module_id, info)` (the loader removes it on unload) or globally
with `Register(info)`. `Import(source, properties, &error)` runs the importer
callback; the editor writes a `.masset` with the resulting type.

**Component snapshots and stable ids.** @ref Mercury::TypeRegistry serializes
a component for replication/save:
`SerializeComponent(scene, entity, "Transform", properties)` and
`DeserializeComponent(..., properties, ComponentDeserializeOptions{})`.
The byte-buffer overload is the network form; it rejects non-finite values and
read-only fields by default. Entities have a stable id:
`scene.Objects().GetGuid(entity)` and `FindByGuid(guid)`. Map entities adopt
their `MapNode` uid (so ids survive reloads); runtime-created entities use a
separate high-range counter. `SetGuid` refuses an id already owned by another
live entity.

**RenderHooks and Filament.** @ref Mercury::RenderHooks receives the live
`filament::Engine` and `filament::View` just before the frame is submitted:

@code{.cpp}
#include <filament/View.h>
#include "application/render_hooks.h"

const int hook = Mercury::RenderHooks::Instance().AddPreRender(
    [](filament::Engine&, filament::View& view) {
      auto bloom = view.getBloomOptions();
      bloom.enabled = true;
      bloom.strength = 0.3f;
      view.setBloomOptions(bloom);
    });
// Remove(hook) when the feature goes away.
@endcode

For engine/renderer/scene/camera access outside a hook, a scene running on
@ref Mercury::FilamentApplication can use `GetEngine()`, `GetRenderer()`,
`GetScene()`, `GetView()` and `GetCamera()`. Hook on the render thread.

**Linking other libraries.** A project's CMakeLists is ordinary CMake. Link a
library you ship with the game after `mercury_add_game`:

@code{.cmake}
mercury_add_game(my_game PROJECT "${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}")
find_package(enet CONFIG REQUIRED)          # or add_subdirectory(third_party/enet)
target_link_libraries(my_game PRIVATE enet::enet)
@endcode

The same pattern works for a vendored library with
`add_subdirectory(third_party/mylib)` and
`target_link_libraries(my_game PRIVATE mylib)`.

@section cpp_api_editor_systems Editor extensions

Editor tools are C++ modules the editor loads while a project is open; they
get the real wxWidgets objects. Put them in `system/`; `mercury_add_editor_system`
builds a MODULE that resolves engine/editor/wx symbols from the running editor.

@code{.cpp}
// system/my_tool.h
#pragma once
#include <extension.h>

class MyTool : public Mercury::EditorUI::EditorExtension {
 public:
  void OnLoad(Mercury::EditorUI::EditorContext& context) override;
};
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
// system/my_tool.cc
#include "my_tool.h"
#include <console.h>

MERCURY_EDITOR_EXTENSION(MyTool);

void MyTool::OnLoad(Mercury::EditorUI::EditorContext& context) {
  auto* console = &context.GetConsole();
  context.AddMenuItem({.menu = "Extensions", .label = "My Tool",
                       .callback = [console] { console->Info("Hello"); }});
}
@endcode

`EditorContext` registers every kind of extension:
`AddPanel` (dockable panel with a factory),
`AddMenuItem` (top-level menu entry),
`AddInspector` (custom Inspector for a component type),
`AddProjectMenuItem` (Project Browser context menu),
`AddViewportTool` (mouse/key input plus an overlay in the Scene view), and
`AddAssetImporter` (owned by the module). Everything is removed when the
module unloads.

**The Bind rule.** Never call `wxEvtHandler::Bind` directly on an
editor-owned wx object with a lambda from the module: after a reload the
handler would point into an unmapped library. Use `context.Bind(handler,
event, callback)`; the loader unbinds before destroying the module's windows.
For a top-level window the module creates (a frame or dialog), call
`context.TrackWindow(window)` so the editor destroys it while the module is
still mapped.

**Reload and versions.** `Project > Reload Systems` (or the project's
`system/` changing) unloads, rebuilds and reloads the module. The build runs in
the background. The module exports `mercury_editor_extension_v1` with an ABI
version and the engine version; the loader rejects an incompatible ABI or a
different major.minor version. Exceptions from a module callback are caught
and logged; after repeated failures the module is faulted and no longer
called. Reload/unload requests made from inside a callback are deferred until
the callback returns.

@section cpp_api_trust Workspace trust

Native builds and editor modules execute project-supplied code (CMake
configure scripts, compiled executables), so they are gated by a persisted
workspace trust decision:

- Opening a project, reading its data and writing the generated LSP/CMake
  files is always allowed. Configuring/building the project tree, running its
  executable, and building `system/` modules require trust.
- The first time a project needs a native build the editor asks. The decision
  is stored per canonical project path in `~/.config/mercury/editor.cfg`
  (or `%APPDATA%\Mercury\editor.cfg`) under `[trust] trusted` / `distrusted`.
- Granting trust for the first time deletes any shipped `.mercury/build` and
  `.mercury/systems` before the first build. After that, a build folder must
  carry the editor's marker; unknown build output is refused.
- Change or revoke a decision with **Project > Trust Editor Systems**.
  Play/Build ask again for an untrusted project, and the build layer itself
  refuses to run.

A command-line run (`./my_game project.mproj`) is an explicit user action and
is not gated.

@section cpp_api_sdk SDK and language server

A packaged editor carries a relocatable SDK in
`Mercury.app/Contents/Resources/sdk` (or the install prefix):
`include/mercury`, `include/editor`, third-party headers under `include/`,
static archives under `lib/`, and `cmake/MercuryConfig.cmake`.
`find_package(Mercury CONFIG REQUIRED)` provides `mercury_add_game` and
`mercury_add_editor_system`.

The editor generates the project's build files (once) and LSP files (every
open):
- `.clangd` sets `CompilationDatabase: .mercury/build/debug` and adds the SDK
  include dirs, `src/`, `system/` and the public defines. If the database does
  not exist yet, clangd falls back to these flags.
- `compile_commands.json` at the project root is a link to
  `.mercury/build/debug/compile_commands.json`, created/refreshed only after a
  configure this editor ran.
- The editor configures/builds the project's own tree in
  `.mercury/build/debug` (Debug) or `.mercury/build/release` (Build Game),
  passing `-DMercury_DIR=<running SDK>/cmake`. Configure only reruns when the
  cache is missing or `CMakeLists.txt`/`CMakePresets.json` changed.

For a command-line build of a project against a packaged SDK:

@code{.sh}
export MERCURY_SDK=/Applications/Mercury.app/Contents/Resources/sdk
cd my_game
cmake --preset debug -DMercury_DIR="$MERCURY_SDK/cmake"
cmake --build --preset debug
@endcode

The `Mercury_DIR` cache variable in the generated presets reads
`$env{MERCURY_SDK}/cmake`; pass `-DMercury_DIR` to override it. A Vulkan SDK
(headers and loader) is required at configure time; the packaged editor ships
the MoltenVK runtime, but C++ builds still need `find_package(Vulkan)` to
succeed. `scripts/setup_project.pl` installs it on a development machine.

@section cpp_api_new_script The New Script dialog

**Create > Script...** offers four kinds (the Project Browser also has direct
**C++ System...** and **Editor System...** entries, and **Add Component > New
Script...** limits the choice to gameplay components):

| Kind | Files | Registered by |
|---|---|---|
| AngelScript Behaviour | `scripts/<snake>.as` | `[script]` |
| C++ Behaviour | `src/<snake>.h`, `src/<snake>.cc` | `MERCURY_SCRIPT` |
| C++ System | `src/<snake>.h`, `src/<snake>.cc` | `MERCURY_SYSTEM` |
| C++ Editor System | `system/<snake>.h`, `system/<snake>.cc` | `MERCURY_EDITOR_EXTENSION` |

The dialog shows the exact files, validates the class name, and disables
Create if either file exists. **Open after creating** opens the `.cc` (or the
`.as` in the built-in editor); the choice and the last kind are remembered.
Creating C++ files gives the project a default `[build] target` when it has
none, and (for trusted projects) reconfigures the build tree in the background
so clangd sees the new file immediately. The next Play/Build compiles it.

@section cpp_api_editor The editor

The editor is also C++. @ref Mercury::EditorUI::MercuryEditorApp is the
`wxApp`; it owns a headless `Mercury::DefaultApplication` and an
@ref Mercury::EditorScene (the map-editing controller), and switches
between @ref Mercury::EditorUI::LauncherFrame and
@ref Mercury::EditorUI::EditorFrame. The panels and document editors are
under `editor/`. The editor is built separately:

@code{.sh}
cmake --build --preset release --target mercury_editor
./build/release/bin/mercury_editor examples/pong/pong.mproj
@endcode

@section cpp_api_paths Include paths

The engine library adds `mercury/` to the include path, so headers are
included from the engine root:

@code{.cpp}
#include "application/default_application.h"
#include "scene/map_scene.h"
#include "scripting/behaviour.h"
#include "project/project.h"
@endcode

The generated API reference lists every public header, namespace and class.
Start from the <a href="namespaces.html">Namespaces</a> page or search for a
type in the search box.

*/
